---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import S from '../components/S.astro';
import L from '../components/L.astro';
import Cite from '../components/Cite.astro';
import Callout from '../components/Callout.astro';
import Widget from '../components/Widget.astro';
import SpecTable from '../components/SpecTable.astro';
---
<Base
  title="Roadmap"
  description="GB300 today, Vera Rubin NVL144 announced for 2H 2026, and the rack power curve underneath all of it — with announced figures clearly separated from measured ones."
  kicker="Chapter 10"
  heading="Roadmap"
  slug="/roadmap"
>
  <div class="prose">
    <p class="lede">
      One rule governs this chapter: anything that has not shipped is labelled as announced, and
      figures taken from keynote slides and supply-chain reporting are not presented next to
      measured benchmarks as though they were the same kind of thing.
    </p>
  </div>

  <Widget
    title="Generations"
    hint="Announced entries are marked"
    caption="Positions are approximate. Solid markers are shipping products with published specifications; hollow markers with a dashed rule are announced roadmap entries whose figures come from keynote material and supply-chain reporting."
  >
    <svg class="diagram" viewBox="0 0 720 250" role="img"
      aria-label="Timeline from GB200 NVL72 through GB300 NVL72 to the announced Vera Rubin NVL144, Rubin Ultra NVL576 and Feynman.">
      <line x1="40" y1="120" x2="690" y2="120" class="axis"/>

      <g>
        <circle cx="90" cy="120" r="7" fill="var(--copper)"/>
        <text x="90" y="100" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl-lg">GB200 NVL72</text>
        <text x="90" y="146" text-anchor="middle">Blackwell</text>
        <text x="90" y="160" text-anchor="middle">192 GB · ~120 kW</text>
        <text x="90" y="174" text-anchor="middle">1.44 EF FP4</text>
        <text x="90" y="196" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink-3)">shipping</text>
      </g>

      <g>
        <circle cx="240" cy="120" r="7" fill="var(--copper)"/>
        <text x="240" y="100" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl-lg">GB300 NVL72</text>
        <text x="240" y="146" text-anchor="middle">Blackwell Ultra</text>
        <text x="240" y="160" text-anchor="middle">288 GB · ~1,400 W/GPU</text>
        <text x="240" y="174" text-anchor="middle">1.5× FP4</text>
        <text x="240" y="196" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink-3)">shipping</text>
      </g>

      <line x1="310" y1="120" x2="690" y2="120" stroke="var(--amber)" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="6 5"/>

      <g>
        <circle cx="400" cy="120" r="7" fill="none" stroke="var(--amber)" stroke-width="2"/>
        <text x="400" y="100" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl-lg">Vera Rubin NVL144</text>
        <text x="400" y="146" text-anchor="middle">Rubin · HBM4</text>
        <text x="400" y="160" text-anchor="middle">~3.6 EF FP4</text>
        <text x="400" y="174" text-anchor="middle">144 dies = 72 packages</text>
        <text x="400" y="196" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--amber)">announced · 2H 2026</text>
      </g>

      <g>
        <circle cx="550" cy="120" r="7" fill="none" stroke="var(--amber)" stroke-width="2"/>
        <text x="550" y="100" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl-lg">Rubin Ultra NVL576</text>
        <text x="550" y="196" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--amber)">announced · 2027</text>
      </g>

      <g>
        <circle cx="670" cy="120" r="7" fill="none" stroke="var(--amber)" stroke-width="2"/>
        <text x="670" y="100" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl-lg">Feynman</text>
        <text x="670" y="196" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--amber)">announced</text>
      </g>
    </svg>
  </Widget>

  <div class="prose">
    <h2 id="gb300">GB300, which is shipping</h2>

    <L min={2}>
      <p>
        Blackwell Ultra keeps the rack shape and changes what is in the trays:
        <S id="gb300-hbm" /> of HBM3e per GPU against 192 GB, <S id="gb300-power" /> per GPU
        against roughly 1,200 W, and <S id="gb300-fp4" /> the FP4 throughput. Scale-out moves from
        ConnectX-7 at <S id="cx7" bare /> to ConnectX-8 at <S id="cx8" bare />.<Cite ids="nv-gb300" />
      </p>
      <p>
        The capacity increase is the consequential one. Fifty percent more HBM per GPU is fifty
        percent more <a href="/inference#dynamo">KV cache and batch headroom</a> on the decode
        pool, which is where memory-bound serving actually converts capacity into throughput.
        NVIDIA reports <S id="mlperf-ultra" /> on MLPerf Training at 512-GPU
        scale.<Cite ids="nv-devblog-mlperf" />
      </p>
    </L>

    <Callout kind="caveat">
      <p>
        Per-GPU memory on Blackwell Ultra is quoted as both 279 GB and 288 GB depending on the
        source — SKU and ECC accounting again. Shown here as <S id="gb300-hbm" /> with the range
        attached, like every other figure the sources disagree about.
      </p>
    </Callout>

    <h2 id="rubin">Vera Rubin, which is not</h2>

    <p>
      Announced for the second half of 2026: Rubin GPUs with HBM4, a Vera CPU, and roughly
      <S id="rubin-fp4" /> of FP4 in an NVL144 rack.
    </p>

    <L min={2}>
      <p>
        The naming deserves a note, because it breaks the pattern the previous two generations set.
        "NVL72" counted 72 GPU packages. "NVL144" counts 144 <em>dies</em> across 72 packages — the
        same package count as today, with the die-per-package doubling now surfaced in the product
        name. It is not a doubling of the NVLink domain's endpoint count, and reading it as one
        will produce wrong capacity planning.
      </p>
      <p>
        Rubin Ultra NVL576 is projected for 2027 and Feynman beyond that. These come from keynote
        slides and supply-chain reporting rather than from datasheets, and this site does not
        publish specifications for them.
      </p>
    </L>

    <h2 id="power">The curve underneath</h2>

    <p>
      The through-line across every generation is not FLOPS. It is watts per rack, and it is the
      constraint that will decide how far this architecture goes.
    </p>

    <L min={2}>
      <p>
        <S id="rack-power" /> today, against a worldwide mean rack density of
        <S id="mean-rack" />.<Cite ids="uptime-2025" /> Schneider Electric's forward-looking figure
        for the next generation is <S id="next-gen-power" /> per rack.<Cite ids="schneider" /> At
        that density the facility, not the silicon, becomes the thing being designed: power
        delivery, coolant volume, floor loading, and grid interaction all move onto the critical
        path.
      </p>
      <p>
        Everything this site describes about NVL72 — the busbar, the copper spine, the mandatory
        liquid loop, the 1.36-tonne frame — is a set of answers to that curve at 120 kW. Each
        subsequent generation is the same question asked again at a larger number.
      </p>
    </L>

    <SpecTable
      caption="Successors"
      ids={['gb300-hbm', 'gb300-power', 'gb300-fp4', 'cx8', 'mlperf-ultra', 'rubin-fp4', 'next-gen-power']}
    />

    <Callout kind="caveat">
      <p>
        Every roadmap figure on this page is announced rather than measured, and is marked as such
        wherever it appears. See <a href="/sources#announced">the announced-figures list</a> for
        all of them in one place.
      </p>
    </Callout>
  </div>
</Base>
